Yes. `scrambler_stm32.hex` is also a **generated firmware file**. You should not manually write its contents.

For your project:

```text
STM32F407_Cortex-M4/
└── build/
    ├── scrambler_stm32.elf
    ├── scrambler_stm32.hex   ← this
    ├── scrambler_stm32.bin
    └── scrambler_stm32.map
```

## What is `scrambler_stm32.hex`?

It is the **Intel HEX representation of your STM32F407 firmware**.

The flow is:

```text
Source Code
    │
    ▼
*.o
    │
    ▼
scrambler_stm32.elf
    │
    │ arm-none-eabi-objcopy
    ▼
scrambler_stm32.hex
```

Your Makefile should have:

```makefile
build/$(TARGET).hex: build/$(TARGET).elf
	@echo " HEX  $@"
	$(OBJCOPY) -O ihex $< $@
```

With:

```makefile
TARGET = scrambler_stm32
OBJCOPY = arm-none-eabi-objcopy
```

So running:

```bash
make
```

automatically creates:

```text
build/scrambler_stm32.hex
```

## What does the HEX file look like?

It contains records similar to:

```text
:020000040800F2
:1000000000100020090100080D01000811010008...
:1000100015010008190100081D01000821010008...
...
:00000001FF
```

These are **address/data records**, not C source code.

The important point is that the HEX file represents where your firmware should be programmed in STM32 Flash.

For STM32F407, internal Flash starts at:

```text
0x08000000
```

So conceptually:

```text
scrambler_stm32.hex
        │
        ├── Flash address
        ├── Firmware data
        ├── Flash address
        ├── Firmware data
        └── End record
```

## HEX vs BIN vs ELF

| File   | Use                                              |
| ------ | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `.elf` | Debugging, symbols, linker information           |
| `.hex` | Programming tools / address-aware firmware image |
| `.bin` | Raw firmware binary                              |
| `.map` | Memory and symbol analysis                       |

For example:

```text
                 ELF
                  │
          ┌───────┴────────┐
          ▼                ▼
        HEX                BIN
          │                │
          └───────┬────────┘
                  ▼
             STM32F407
                Flash
             0x08000000
```

### Verify your HEX file

After:

```bash
make
```

run:

```bash
ls -lh build/scrambler_stm32.hex
```

You can also inspect it:

```bash
head build/scrambler_stm32.hex
```

And verify the generated images from the ELF:

```bash
arm-none-eabi-objcopy -O ihex build/scrambler_stm32.elf build/scrambler_stm32.hex
arm-none-eabi-objcopy -O binary build/scrambler_stm32.elf build/scrambler_stm32.bin
```

**For your project, `scrambler_stm32.hex` should therefore be generated by the Makefile—not manually created.**

